---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy Policy" description="How Calorie Cownter handles your data.">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h1 class="section-title" style="margin-bottom: 8px;">Privacy Policy</h1>

      <p class="updated-note">
        Last updated <strong>September 19, 2026</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Calorie Cownter is an iPhone app for tracking the food you eat. This
        policy explains what we collect, what we don't, and where your data
        actually lives. We've tried to keep it plain — if anything is unclear,
        email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>There is <strong>no account and no sign-in</strong> — not even for the AI features.</li>
        <li>Your food diary lives in <strong>your own iCloud</strong>, not on our servers.</li>
        <li>Your <strong>Apple Health data never leaves your phone</strong>.</li>
        <li>AI meal photos are analyzed and then <strong>not stored</strong>.</li>
        <li>There are <strong>no ads</strong>, and <strong>no analytics or tracking in the app</strong>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>No account, no sign-in</h2>
      <p>
        There is no sign-up, no sign-in, and no password anywhere in Calorie
        Cownter. You never give us an email address or a name, and we never ask
        for one. Open the app and start logging.
      </p>
      <p>
        The AI features do need a way to tell one device apart from another, so
        we can count the free daily scans and know whether a subscription is
        active. Your device uses Apple's <strong>StoreKit</strong> to produce an
        anonymous identifier for this app (Apple calls it the app transaction
        ID), and our backend verifies it with Apple and stores it. That
        identifier is specific to this app — it isn't your Apple Account, it
        doesn't tell us who you are, and it can't be used to look you up
        anywhere else. It's the only thing we store about you.
      </p>

      <h2>AI meal scanning</h2>
      <p>
        When you scan a meal by photo or by typing a description, that photo or
        text is sent to Calorie Cownter's backend server, which forwards it to a
        third-party AI provider (<strong>Anthropic / Claude</strong>) to estimate
        the calories and macros.
      </p>
      <p>
        The image is processed transiently to generate an estimate. It is
        <strong>not stored</strong> on our server or kept by us after the
        analysis is returned to your phone.
      </p>

      <h2>Barcode scanning</h2>
      <p>
        When you scan a barcode, the barcode number is sent to our backend and
        looked up in the <strong>Open Food Facts</strong> public database. No
        account is needed and no AI is involved.
      </p>

      <h2>Subscriptions</h2>
      <p>
        Calorie Cow Pro, the optional subscription for unlimited AI scans, is sold
        and managed entirely by <strong>Apple / StoreKit</strong> through
        RevenueCat. Our backend receives subscription status events keyed to the
        same anonymous app transaction ID described above, so we know whether a
        subscription is active. <strong>No payment or card details ever reach
        us.</strong>
      </p>

      <h2>Usage logging</h2>
      <p>
        To enforce the free daily AI limit and keep the service running, our
        backend keeps a minimal per-scan usage record: counts, the AI model used,
        token counts, an estimated cost, timing, your app version, and OS version.
        This ledger is about <em>usage</em> — it does <strong>not</strong> contain
        your food diary and does <strong>not</strong> contain the image you scanned.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your device / in your iCloud</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your entire food diary</strong> — everything you log is stored
          on your device and synced through your <strong>private iCloud
          database</strong>, across your own devices only. We have no access to it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>All Apple Health data</strong> — if you connect Apple Health,
          those values stay on your phone. The app only keeps a local "connected"
          flag; <strong>no Health values are sent anywhere</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>No ads or tracking</h2>
      <p>
        Calorie Cownter contains no advertising, no ad networks, and no
        third-party analytics or tracking SDKs. Nothing you log is ever measured
        or reported.
      </p>
      <p>
        This website (caloriecownter.com) is the one exception, and a small one.
        It uses <a href="https://umami.is/" rel="noopener">Umami</a> to count
        page views so we can tell whether anyone is finding the site. Umami sets
        no cookies, stores no personal data, and can't follow you to other
        sites. We see totals such as how many people opened a page and which
        country they were in, never who you are. It runs only on this website,
        never in the app.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Calorie Cownter is not directed at children under 13, and we don't
        knowingly collect personal information from them.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, we'll update this page and the "last updated" date
        above.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy? Email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
